# 🛠️ How I Developed the Final Output

Creating the final poster was not simply about putting pictures and text together.

I started by thinking about the **main purpose of the advertisement**.

The most important message was:

> **There is FREE coffee, and people should come get it.**

From there, I decided what elements were necessary and what elements could be removed.

### Step 1 — Establishing the Concept

I first decided that the poster should have a **minimalist coffee-shop aesthetic**.

I wanted a clean background and a limited number of visual elements so the advertisement would not feel overcrowded.

---

### Step 2 — Choosing the Main Visual

The iced coffee became the main visual because it immediately communicates the product.

I made it significantly larger than the supporting elements to establish a clear focal point.

---

### Step 3 — Adding Supporting Elements

After establishing the main visual, I added:

- Coffee beans
- Bread
- School logo
- Promotional text
- Event date

Each element was added with a purpose.

I avoided adding unnecessary decorations because they could distract from the main message.

---

### Step 4 — Creating Visual Hierarchy

I arranged the text and images according to importance.

The viewer should notice the promotional message and coffee first, followed by the event information.

This helped make the poster understandable even when viewed quickly.

---

### Step 5 — Choosing the Color Palette

I used warm coffee-inspired colors to create consistency throughout the design.

The brown and beige tones connect the different visual elements and make the poster feel cohesive.

---

### Step 6 — Refining the Layout

I adjusted the size, position, spacing, and orientation of the different elements until the composition felt balanced.

The goal was not to make everything perfectly symmetrical.

Instead, I wanted the layout to feel **natural, playful, and dynamic**, similar to the casual feeling of enjoying coffee.

---

### Step 7 — Adding the Final Message

Finally, I added the closing statement:

> **"Free coffee. No catch. Just caffeine and good vibes. ☕ - first come, first served."**

This line gives the advertisement personality.

Instead of ending with a formal promotional statement, it ends with something more conversational and fun.

It makes the poster feel more human.

